<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>1.13.2: Creating Tables from Verbal Descriptions</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00286</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.13.2: Creating Tables from Verbal Descriptions</md:title>
    <md:uuid>f7b67e6a-e513-4b92-9980-d90df58fabda</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>Creating a Table of Values</title>
      <para id="para-00001">For each situation in numbers 1 – 3, create a table of values with at least five columns to represent the situation.</para>
      <!--Q#1-->
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00002">Julio works at a clothing store and makes $14 per hour.</para>
          <para id="para-00003">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00004">Compare your answer: </para>
          <table class="unnumbered os-raise-horizontaltable" id="table-00001">
            <tgroup cols="7">
            
  <tbody>
                <row>
                  <entry><emphasis>Number of hours</emphasis></entry>
                  <entry>0</entry>
                  <entry>1</entry>
                  <entry>2</entry>
                  <entry>3</entry>
                  <entry>4</entry>
                  <entry>5</entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry><emphasis>Amount earned</emphasis></entry>
                  <entry>0</entry>
                  <entry>14</entry>
                  <entry>28</entry>
                  <entry>42</entry>
                  <entry>56</entry>
                  <entry>70</entry>
                </row>
              </tbody>
            </tgroup>
          </table>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#-->
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00005">Jake had cavities at his last dentist visit. The dentist told him to brush his teeth for two minutes each time he brushes.</para>
          <para id="para-00006">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00007">Compare your answer: </para>
          <table class="unnumbered os-raise-horizontaltable unnumbered" id="table-00002">
            <tgroup cols="6">
            
  <tbody>
                <row>
                  <entry><emphasis>Amount earned</emphasis></entry>
                  <entry>1</entry>
                  <entry>2</entry>
                  <entry>3</entry>
                  <entry>4</entry>
                  <entry>5</entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry><emphasis>Total number of minutes brushed</emphasis></entry>
                  <entry>2</entry>
                  <entry>4</entry>
                  <entry>6</entry>
                  <entry>8</entry>
                  <entry>10</entry>
                </row>
              </tbody>
            </tgroup>
          </table>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#3-->
      <exercise id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00008">For a fundraiser, Celia ran 5 miles on Monday (day 0) and then 3 miles every day after that.</para>
          <para id="para-00009">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00010">Compare your answer: </para>
          <table class="unnumbered os-raise-horizontaltable" id="table-00003">
            <tgroup cols="7">
            
  <tbody>
                <row>
                  <entry><emphasis><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math></emphasis></entry>
                  <entry>0</entry>
                  <entry>1</entry>
                  <entry>2</entry>
                  <entry>3</entry>
                  <entry>4</entry>
                  <entry>5</entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry><emphasis><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math></emphasis></entry>
                  <entry>5</entry>
                  <entry>8</entry>
                  <entry>11</entry>
                  <entry>14</entry>
                  <entry>17</entry>
                  <entry>20</entry>
                </row>
              </tbody>
            </tgroup>
          </table>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <section id="sect-00002">
        <title>Using a Table of Values to Write Equations</title>
        <para id="para-00011">Table of values can be used to write equations. The tables from <emphasis>Creating a Table of Values</emphasis> all represent linear equations.</para>
        <para id="para-00012">For 4 - 8, revisit the table from question 1 and write an equation to represent the table of values. Notice that columns for the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> values are added.</para>
        <table class="unnumbered os-raise-horizontaltable" id="table-00004">
          <tgroup cols="7">
          
  <tbody>
              <row>
                <entry><emphasis><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math></emphasis></entry>
                <entry>0</entry>
                <entry>1</entry>
                <entry>2</entry>
                <entry>3</entry>
                <entry>4</entry>
                <entry>5</entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry><emphasis><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math></emphasis></entry>
                <entry>0</entry>
                <entry>14</entry>
                <entry>28</entry>
                <entry>42</entry>
                <entry>56</entry>
                <entry>70</entry>
              </row>
            </tbody>
          </tgroup>
        </table>
      </section>
      <exercise id="_7a5095fe-59e3-4bcf-9f11-a5bdfa8e1c46" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00013">What is the starting value (when <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>, what does <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> equal)?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00014">Correct! <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
          <para id="para-00015">The correct answer is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Q#5-->
      <exercise id="exer-00004">
        <problem id="prob-00005">
          <para id="para-00016">How would you describe this value on the graph?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00005">
          <para id="para-00017">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00018"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#6-->
      <exercise id="exer-00005">
        <problem id="prob-00006">
          <para id="para-00019">What is the rate of change, or the change between the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-values over the change between the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-values? <media alt="Placeholder for Alt Text">
              <iframe width="660" height="371.4" src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/nZiRu4PpXDo?si=5Rg6NDyzCgjfNHwO;&amp;rel=0"/>
            </media></para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00006">
          <para id="para-00020">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00021"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>14</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math> or 14</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#7-->
      <exercise id="exer-00006">
        <problem id="prob-00007">
          <para id="para-00022">How would you describe this value on the graph?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00007">
          <para id="para-00023">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00024">slope</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#8-->
      <exercise id="exer-00007">
        <problem id="prob-00008">
          <para id="para-00025">Using the information in questions 4 – 7, what would the equation be for the values represented by the table? Use slope-intercept form.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00008">
          <para id="para-00026">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00027"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>14</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> or <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>14</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math></para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#9-->
      <exercise id="exer-00008">
        <problem id="prob-00009">
          <para id="para-00028">Write an equation from the table of values created in question 2 of <emphasis>Creating a Table of Values</emphasis>.</para>
          <para id="para-00029">Hint: (Work backward to find the value where <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> using the pattern set in the table of values).</para>
          <table class="unnumbered os-raise-horizontaltable" id="table-00005">
            <tgroup cols="7">
            
  <tbody>
                <row>
                  <entry><emphasis><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math></emphasis></entry>
                  <entry>0</entry>
                  <entry>1</entry>
                  <entry>2</entry>
                  <entry>3</entry>
                  <entry>4</entry>
                  <entry>5</entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry><emphasis><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math></emphasis></entry>
                  <entry>?</entry>
                  <entry>2</entry>
                  <entry>4</entry>
                  <entry>6</entry>
                  <entry>8</entry>
                  <entry>10</entry>
                </row>
              </tbody>
            </tgroup>
          </table>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00009">
          <para id="para-00030">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00031"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> (note: subtract the top row by 1 and the bottom row by 2 to go back to where <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> for the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#10-->
      <exercise id="exer-00009">
        <problem id="prob-00010">
          <para id="para-00032">Write an equation from the table of values created in question 3 of <emphasis>Creating a Table of Values</emphasis></para>
          <table class="unnumbered os-raise-horizontaltable" id="table-00006">
            <tgroup cols="7">
            
  <tbody>
                <row>
                  <entry><emphasis><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math></emphasis></entry>
                  <entry>0</entry>
                  <entry>1</entry>
                  <entry>2</entry>
                  <entry>3</entry>
                  <entry>4</entry>
                  <entry>5</entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry><emphasis><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math></emphasis></entry>
                  <entry>?</entry>
                  <entry>2</entry>
                  <entry>4</entry>
                  <entry>6</entry>
                  <entry>8</entry>
                  <entry>10</entry>
                </row>
              </tbody>
            </tgroup>
          </table>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00010">
          <para id="para-00033">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00034"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para></solution>
      </exercise></section>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <section id="sect-00003">
        <title>Video: Writing an Equation from a Table of Values</title>
        <para id="para-00035">Watch the following video to learn more about writing equations from a table of values.</para>
        <media alt="Placeholder for Alt Text">
          <iframe width="660" height="371.4" src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/nZiRu4PpXDo?si=OEfKjtDOzQiG_BR2;&amp;rel=0"/>
        </media>
      </section>
      <section id="sect-00004">
        <title>Create Your Own Situation</title>
        <para id="para-00036">Think of your own situation that could be modeled by a linear equation. Create a table of values that represents that situation. Write your problem down then switch with a partner. Make a table of values for each other’s problems. Then, check your work together.</para>
        <para id="para-00037">Next, plot the points of each table of values in a coordinate plane. Answer the questions after discussing with your partner:</para>
        <!--Q#1-->
        <exercise id="exer-00010">
          <problem id="prob-00011">
            <para id="para-00038">Describe the scenario created by your partner.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00011">
            <para id="para-00039">Compare your answer: </para>
            <para id="para-00040">Answers vary.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <!--Interaction End -->
        <!--Q#2-->
        <exercise id="exer-00011">
          <problem id="prob-00012">
            <para id="para-00041"> Use Desmos to graph the table of values to represent your partner’s scenario.</para>
            <para id="para-00042">Use the graphing tool or technology outside the course. Graph the equation that represents this scenario using the Desmos tool below.</para>
            <media alt="insert alt text">
              <iframe src="../../media/desmos/a47b5bb4ba783be55a7067b919f165f14dcc36ce.html" width="500" height="400"/>
            </media>
            <!--QUESTION IS OUTSIDE OF INTERACTION DUE TO DESMOS-->
            <para id="para-00043">When you have finished graphing, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00012">
            <para id="para-00044">Compare your answer: </para>
            <para id="para-00045">Answers vary.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <!--Interaction End -->
        <!--Q#3-->
        <exercise id="exer-00012">
          <problem id="prob-00013">
            <para id="para-00046">Does your partner’s data model a linear relationship or not? Describe how you determined your answer.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00013">
            <para id="para-00047">Compare your answer: </para>
            <para id="para-00048">The points form a line. This happens because the same number was added to each value in the top row and the same number was added to each value in the bottom row of the tables.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <!--Interaction End -->
      </section>
      <!-- "Start "Are you Ready for More? click to reveal.  If you have more than one on a page, you will need to change the Data-fire/data-wait-for-events for each set.-->
      <section id="sect-00005">
        <title>Are you ready for more?</title>
        <section class="numbered-exercises" id="sect-00006"><title>Extending Your Thinking</title>
          <exercise class="RFM1" id="exer-00013">
            <problem id="prob-00014">
              <para id="para-00049">Kylie pays $20 to rent a scooter plus $0.40 per mile she drives the scooter. Create a table of values to represent Kylie’s situation starting with <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
            </problem>
            <solution id="sol-00014">
              <para id="para-00050">Compare your answer: </para>
              <table class="unnumbered os-raise-midsizetable" id="table-00007">
                <tgroup cols="6">
                
  <tbody>
                    <row>
                      <entry><emphasis>0</emphasis></entry>
                      <entry><emphasis>1</emphasis></entry>
                      <entry><emphasis>2</emphasis></entry>
                      <entry><emphasis>3</emphasis></entry>
                      <entry><emphasis>4</emphasis></entry>
                      <entry><emphasis>5</emphasis></entry>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                      <entry>20</entry>
                      <entry>20.40</entry>
                      <entry>20.80</entry>
                      <entry>21.20</entry>
                      <entry>21.60</entry>
                      <entry>22.10</entry>
                    </row>
                  </tbody>
                </tgroup>
              </table>
            </solution>
          </exercise>
          <!--End interaction-->
          <exercise class="RFM1" id="exer-00014">
            <problem id="prob-00015">
              <para id="para-00051">Use the table to determine how much she has to pay after 5 miles.</para>
            </problem>
            <solution id="sol-00015">
              <para id="para-00052">Compare your answer: </para>
              <para id="para-00053">Kylie would pay $22.10.</para>
            </solution>
          </exercise>
          <!--End interaction-->
        </section>
    </section>
    <note class="self-check" id="note-00001">
      <exercise id="exer-00016">
        <problem id="prob-00017">
        <para id="para-00054"><link class="os-embed" url="#exercise/Alg1_1_13_2_SC"/></para>
        </problem>
        </exercise>
    </note>
    <section id="sect-00007">
      <title>Additional Resources</title>
      <section id="sect-00008">
        <title>Writing Linear Equations from Tables</title>
        <para id="para-00055">When writing linear equations from tables, it is important to identify the slope and the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercepts from the table.</para>
        <para id="para-00056"><emphasis>Example</emphasis></para>
        <para id="para-00057">Given the table, write an equation in slope-intercept form.</para>
        <table class="unnumbered os-raise-horizontaltable" id="table-00009">
          <tgroup cols="6">
          
  <tbody>
              <row>
                <entry><emphasis><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math></emphasis></entry>
                <entry>0</entry>
                <entry>1</entry>
                <entry>2</entry>
                <entry>3</entry>
                <entry>4</entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry><emphasis><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math></emphasis></entry>
                <entry>12</entry>
                <entry>16</entry>
                <entry>20</entry>
                <entry>24</entry>
                <entry>28</entry>
              </row>
            </tbody>
          </tgroup>
        </table>
        <para id="para-00058"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Identify the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept.</para>
        <para id="para-00059">The <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept is the starting value, when <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
        <para id="para-00074">Looking at the table, when <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>, the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-value is 12, so this is the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept.</para>
        <para id="para-00060"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Identify the slope.</para>
        <para id="para-00061">Slope is the change in <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> over the change in <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
        <para id="para-00075">The <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-values change by 4 each time. The <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-values change by 1 each time.</para>
        <para id="para-00062">The slope is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math> or 4.</para>
        <para id="para-00063"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Write the equation in slope-intercept form.</para>
        <para id="para-00064">Slope intercept form is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>m</m:mi><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mi>b</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, where <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is the slope and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept.</para>
        <para id="para-00076">Substitute the values, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>12</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
        
        <note class="try" id="note-00002">
          <title>Try It: Writing Linear Equations from Tables</title>
          <!--Q#-->
      <exercise id="exer-00015">
            <problem id="prob-00016">
              <para id="para-00065">Given the table, write an equation in slope-intercept form.</para>
              <table class="unnumbered os-raise-horizontaltable" id="table-00010">
                <tgroup cols="5">
                
  <tbody>
                    <row>
                      <entry><emphasis><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math></emphasis></entry>
                      <entry>0</entry>
                      <entry>1</entry>
                      <entry>2</entry>
                      <entry>3</entry>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                      <entry><emphasis><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math></emphasis></entry>
                      <entry>6</entry>
                      <entry>11</entry>
                      <entry>16</entry>
                      <entry>21</entry>
                    </row>
                  </tbody>
                </tgroup>
              </table>
            </problem>
            <solution id="sol-00016">
              <para id="para-00066">Compare your answer: </para>
              <para id="para-00067">Here is how to write the equation in slope-intercept form:</para>
              <para id="para-00068"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Identify the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept.</para>
              <para id="para-00069">The <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept is the starting value, when <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>. Looking at the table, when <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>, the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-value is 6, so this is the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept.</para>
              <para id="para-00070"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Identify the slope.</para>
              <para id="para-00071">Slope is the change in <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> over the change in <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>. The <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-values change by 5 each time. The <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-values change by 1 each time. The slope is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math> or 5.</para>
              <para id="para-00072"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Write the equation in slope-intercept form.</para>
              <para id="para-00073">Slope intercept form is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>m</m:mi><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mi>b</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, where <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is the slope and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept. Substitute the values, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
            </solution>
          </exercise></note>
        <!--Interaction End -->
      </section>
    </section>
  </content>
</document>